WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.080
We think of animated shows as harmless kids'
fare, but that isn't always the case.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.050
Each of these cartoon episodes were so raunchy,
so stupid, or so downright confusing that

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.820
they were denied an audience entirely.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:17.060
From its 1994 debut, Gargoyles gained a devoted
fanbase for its complex plots, fluid animation,

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.440
and serious storytelling.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.340
As an action-adventure series chronicling
the lives of gargoyles who come to life at

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.290
night to protect the places and people they
love, the series explored themes including

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.280
frustrated love, urban crime, and corporate
greed.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.640
This willingness to go to intense places made
Gargoyles an enduring hit that lives on in

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:38.270
cosplay and tie-in comics to this day — but
in the minds of network executives, it also

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.120
crossed the line.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.560
The season one episode "Deadly Force" is a
compassionate, albeit stern story about gun

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:48.020
safety, in which fun-loving gargoyle Broadway
accidentally shoots human detective Elisa

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.020
with her own gun.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:54.300
She is badly wounded and barely survives emergency
surgery, and Broadway goes on a frantic, guilt-ridden

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.640
crusade against guns.

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:59.680
Ultimately, a very important lesson is learned
about the safe handling of firearms — but

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.360
not before several seconds of Elisa with a
bullet in her belly filled the screen.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.160
“Oh,"

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.200
"Deadly Force" was pulled from rotation for
a while, and subsequent airings on spinoff

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.090
channels Toon Disney and Disney XD re-cut
the scene so that only Elisa's anguished face

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:16.780
is visible.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.880
Regardless, the Gargoyles team remains proud
of the message they sent about gun violence

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.280
in this episode, and stand by it as a thoughtful
examination of firearms and their mishandling.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.120
Dexter's Lab was one of the biggest successes
of 1990s animation, an irreverent send-up

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.880
of everything from midcentury space-age ambition
to Marvel action heroes.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.340
At the heart of it was Dexter, megalomaniacal
child genius with a secret lab behind his

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.980
bedroom bookcase, and Dee Dee, his goofy,
tutu-clad sister.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.219
Their clashes defined many episodes of the
show and typically went to bizarre extremes,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.500
as when Dexter sought to grow a beard to display
his manliness after Dee Dee's teasing, or

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.980
when Dee Dee inspired a crush in Dexter's
arch-nemesis, Mandark.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.509
That sibling rivalry reached new heights in
"Dexter's Rude Removal."

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:02.909
In this infamous episode, Dexter develops
a "rude removal system" to remove Dee Dee's

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:04.460
more irritating qualities.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.000
This backfires, and Dexter ends up creating
two versions of himself and his sister: a

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.250
polite, vaguely British pair, and a pair whose
every other word is censored by profanity

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:13.740
bleeps.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.400
"Where the f--k are we?”

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.220
“Beats the f--k outta me!"

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.760
Despite its completion, "Dexter's Rude Removal"
never aired.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.640
Series creator Genndy Tartakovsky mentioned
it from time to time, growing its legend,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.660
until finally, it surfaced on late-night programming
block Adult Swim's website.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.840
Despite the fact that no curse word is ever
actually uttered uncensored, the mere fact

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.060
of their existence was too much for delicate
ears.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.760
Tiny Toon Adventures was known for its wry
attitude towards cartoon conventions.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:46.040
Babs, Buster, and the rest of the Acme Looniversity
gang regularly poked fun at the expectations

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.980
and tropes of the medium, from subverting
slapstick gags involving mallets and banana

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.500
peels to the contrasting iconic jokesters
like Bugs Bunny and Daffy Duck with genteel

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.100
mortar boards and academic robes as the Looniversity's
professors.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.360
From the cartoon's setting in a town called
"Acme Acres" to character names like "Mary

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.430
Melody" riffing off Warner Bros.' legendary
"Merrie Melodies" cartoons, the whole cartoon

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.840
rests on a foundation of meta-textual ribbing.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.300
But that went a little off the rails with
the infamously banned "One Beer."

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.689
Plucky Duck, Hamton J. Pig, and Buster Bunny
go on a whirlwind bender when they drink beer

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:21.180
for the first time, quickly plummeting from
wide-eyed kids to stubble-chinned lowlifes.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.780
"You babes wanna hang, or what?"

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.689
Despite the fact that Buster opens the escapade
by outright saying "in this episode, we're

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:34.560
showing the evils of alcohol," and ending
it by asking if they can do a "funny episode

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.880
tomorrow" now that they've lectured the kids,
"One Beer" was banned until 2013 when it was

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.980
finally included in a DVD release.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.280
Let that be a lesson to animators across the
globe: don't try to teach valuable lessons!

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.260
Cow and Chicken never pretended to be anything
but crass.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:53.120
Its titular siblings, excitable second-grader
Cow and hot-tempered fifth-grader Chicken,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.159
regularly got into trouble that made it clear
they were there to make kids laugh, not provide

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.159
moral guardianship.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.780
"Chicken, that's a good way to lose a beak.”

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.360
“Ah, shut up, Cow.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.240
Mind your own beeswax.”

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.960
Take, for example, their enemy Red Guy, who
might have actually been the devil but was

00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:12.660
most notable for not wearing pants: Among
his many aliases were Officer Pantsoffski,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.660
Mrs. Barederriere, Major Wedgie, and NoPants
DeLeon.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.040
No joke was too crude for this cartoon to
indulge.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.240
There was, however, a limit the censors did
not want crossed, and Cow and Chicken found

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.520
it with "Buffalo Gals," seven solid minutes
of lesbian jokes.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.780
Unsurprisingly, it faced resistance from network
executives — although it lives on through

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:30.840
the magic of the internet.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.140
Dudley Do-Right's "Stokey the Bear" was intended
in the spirit of fun.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.850
Canada is roundly, if kindly parodied in the
episode as the home of trees, lumberjacks,

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.340
the firewood industry and… well, not much
else.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.540
Snidely Whiplash is doing his diabolical deeds
to better barbecue his hot dogs.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.380
Stokey was meant to be very much a parody
of Smokey of "Only You Can Prevent Forest

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.419
Fires" fame, but hypnotized by Snidely into
setting fires.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.660
All in good fun, right?

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:55.240
Wrong.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.040
The U.S. Forest Service wasn't too pleased
with the spoof of their beloved mascot, in

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:03.090
no small part because Smokey was created with
a very particular purpose in mind — one

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.620
the episode was deliberately muddling.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.020
Understandable, perhaps, especially given
that the episode's final gag implies Stokey

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.740
was responsible for the Great Chicago Fire
of 1871.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.540
The episode was yanked, Stokey was snuffed,
and it never saw airtime again.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.980
Batman: The Brave and the Bold was a self-conscious
tribute and recreation of Silver Age DC Comics:

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.540
bright, funny, and unabashedly nonsensical.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.240
From the retro aesthetic to capers with titles
like "Invasion of the Secret Santas!," Brave

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:33.040
and the Bold was all about having a good and
goofy time.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.810
Mostly, this resulted in kid-friendly hijinks
of the laser-guns-and-magical-truth-serums

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:38.320
variety.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.320
But in "The Mask of Matches Malone!," Brave
and the Bold got downright naughty.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.419
When they're caught sneaking into a nightclub,
Huntress, Catwoman, and Black Canary think

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:50.919
fast and perform "Birds of Prey," a throwback
toe-tapper about the men of the DC universe

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.220
that stuffs an innuendo into every other line.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:57.940
This ranges from Catwoman purring "what a
weapon, what a bang" about Batman, the implication

00:05:57.949 --> 00:06:01.960
that Green Arrow doesn't always "shoot straight,"
and a complaint about the Flash being "just

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:02.640
too fast."

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.979
but what got the episode booted off the season
2 DVD collection was the suggestive finger

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.330
waggle Huntress deploys when discussing Aquaman.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.860
"His little fish, less outrageous."

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:16.480
With a bit of reanimation to remove the telltale
finger, it was included as a bonus episode

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:21.030
on the season 3 DVD, and thus everyone now
knows all about the male members of the Justice

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.720
League and their prowess — on and off the
battlefield.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.440
Cast your mind back to 1987, and recall the
Garbage Pail Kids trading cards.

00:06:29.449 --> 00:06:33.830
Their success rested entirely upon the fact
that they were utterly disgusting little artifacts

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:38.069
of childhood grotesquerie — a perfect parody
of the apple-cheeked goodness of the Cabbage

00:06:38.069 --> 00:06:38.700
Patch Kids.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.760
With such characters as Adam Bomb, Oozy Suzy,
and Snotwich Sandra, they were pulling no

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.900
punches when it came to grossing kids out
and cracking them up.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:52.039
But the 1987 Garbage Pail Kids cartoon went
too far for the U.S. viewing audience.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:57.620
Starring Split Kit, Clogged Duane, Elliot
Mess, Terri Cloth, and Patty Putty, the eponymous

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.960
kids used their disgusting powers to save
the day.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.550
Groups including Action for Children's Television
and Christian Leaders for Responsible Television

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.760
protested, however, and the show was yanked
from the airwaves mere days before release.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.800
Though it did go on to air in other countries,
it never saw the light of US viewership.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.600
The Garbage Pail Kids were, to be frank, dumped.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.400
The English-language dub of anime megahit
Sailor Moon cut and censored so many things

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.480
from the original Japanese version, it merits
a video of its own.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:28.900
Many of these changes have become infamous:
Sailor Uranus and Sailor Neptune, originally

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.320
lovers, were changed to "cousins," and multiple
instances of implied nudity were scrapped.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.700
Before many of these now-infamous choices
were made, however, was "Day of Destiny,"

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:39.060
the finale to season one.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.620
Originally a two-parter, "Day of Destiny"
sees Sailor Moon and company storm the evil

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.240
Queen Beryl's arctic fortress.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.660
Her henchman attack, and the battle quickly
goes south: Sailors Mercury, Mars, Jupiter,

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:53.970
and Venus all die on the ice, and Sailor Moon
is forced to fight the supercharged Beryl

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:54.600
on her own.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:58.460
Given that the original Japanese title of
the first episode is "Death of the Sailor

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:02.570
Guardians: The Tragic Final Battle," it is
perhaps unsurprising that it was cut, and

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:06.060
the finale reduced to one cobbled-together
episode for the U.S. market.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.820
While it wasn't outright banned, the original
version was just too much for kids.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.820
1990s playgrounds the world over found themselves
beset by a new fad: It was called Pokemon,

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.280
and it still has yet to release its grip on
young minds.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.200
Kids just can't get enough of Pikachu, Squirtle,
and the gang.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.420
But a rumor haunted the franchise early on,
mutating as it passed from kid to kid.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.260
There was an episode, they whispered, that
was banned.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.300
The internet was in its infancy and no one
had ever seen the fabled episode, and so the

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.620
tales grew unchecked.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.660
Even today you might hear a child whisper
about a killer episode of Pokemon that no

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:38.660
one has ever seen.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.140
Unlike other kid-spread legends, this one
really did turn out to be true.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.000
"Electric Soldier Porygon" is the episode
in question, and it was indeed banned from

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.920
the airwaves for reasons of public health.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:52.940
Hundreds of Japanese children were rushed
to the hospital after it aired in 1997, suffering

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.300
from seizures, convulsions and vomiting.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.280
It turned out that a brief moment of bright,
strobing animation induced this illness and

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.640
set off a generation's favorite bit of gossip.

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:05.720
Twenty years later, it still fascinates us
— though luckily, those prone to seizures

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:07.080
know to keep well away.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.900
The Powerpuff Girls was never a show content
to be normal.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:15.590
The very premise itself — man combines sugar,
spice, everything nice, and a mysterious black

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:19.830
ooze, creates triplets — is bizarre, and
episodes hinged on everything from elaborate

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:23.040
Beatles parodies to the sinister effects of
glue consumption.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.480
But in season five's "See Me, Feel Me, Gnomey,"
this was taken to new heights: in an homage

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.370
to rock operas like Tommy, the episode is
a sung-through musical in which the girls

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.500
meet a magical gnome who promises to vanquish
all evil in exchange for their powers.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:37.820
They take his offer, and he makes Townsville
into a cult.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.560
Eventually, the day is saved, Townsville is
restored, and the gnome is defeated.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.600
So why did Cartoon Network keep the episode
off U.S. airwaves?

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.440
Questions swirled for years until show creator
Craig McCracken finally provided some answers

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.470
via his Tumblr:

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.040
"They banned it because they claimed that
the metal beams in the destroyed buildings

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:57.250
looked too much like crosses and one of the
hippies looked like Jesus."

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:00.730
The staff was surprised and the fans were
deprived, but it all worked out in the end:

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:04.721
the episode is now available to anyone who
wants to watch it on Hulu, iTunes, or on the

00:10:04.721 --> 00:10:06.620
original series DVDs.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:13.460
Anime exploded in popularity in the late 1990s,
and Toonami, a Cartoon Network programming

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.940
block aimed at the action-hungry after-school
crowd, was a huge part of why.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:21.280
From Sailor Moon to Dragon Ball Z, Toonami
aired the coolest, edgiest, often weirdest

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.940
cartoons Western kids had ever seen — and
it kept them glued to their TVs.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:29.310
New episodes appeared daily, movie-length
specials were hyped for weeks prior to airing,

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:33.380
and occasionally, Toonami even pushed the
boundaries of what was considered acceptable.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.120
Action was tougher, faster, and more intense
on Toonami, and jokes were a bit more edgy.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:41.560
Outlaw Star, a spacefaring action series,
often pushed that line.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:47.140
But one whole episode was deemed unacceptable,
impossible to edit into suitability, and unceremoniously

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:47.640
dumped.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.960
It's no surprise as to why: "Hot Springs Planet
Tenrei" sees hero Gene Starwind sent to world

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.120
of beautiful saunas filled with even-more-beautiful
— and always unclothed — ladies.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:01.351
In the end, kiddie viewers just had to deal
with the lack of continuity — until, many

00:11:01.351 --> 00:11:06.180
years later, it aired on the late-night reimagined
Toonami, intended for just those kids who'd

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.420
loved it the first time around.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.240
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.980
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
cartoons are coming soon.

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.080
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

